            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "FWIW, I thought you were going to look at MultiPart parsing first, but sure, doing this first is fine as well and is useful for simple update."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/app.hpp",
                    "line": 185,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "App is definitely not the right place for anything that happens on a per-request basis.  ALso, it shouldn't be passing boost types as arguments."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/app.hpp",
                    "line": 185,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "App is definitely not the right place for anything that happens on a per-request basis\nIsn't this approach similar to how \"handle\" is integrated into the app? \n\nALso, it shouldn't be passing boost types as arguments.\n\nThe prepareRequestBody function is called while the parser is still processing. Bmcweb only creates the request object after the parser has finished and been released. Unfortunately, it seems there are no alternatives here other than directly using the Boost type.\n\nThere are still open questions regarding error handling when a read operation fails. Specifically, how should we clean up the serialized content in such cases? Should we proceed by invoking handle (or a similar new callback) with an error, allowing the application to manage the cleanup and error handling?\n\nThe file descriptor provided by the route handler might be obtained after a D-Bus call. This implies that bmcweb's request processing should be restructured. Ideally, reading the request body should begin only after session creation and privilege checks are completed.\n\nYou have any suggestion for it?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_body.hpp",
                    "line": 68,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I would prefer if this API only passed filename back to the users, and this were made a protected type (or a friend of the reader).  If we're using this API, nothing within the process should be using the real file handle, so exposing it to the individual routes just adds the potential for issues.."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_body.hpp",
                    "line": 277,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is making me think we should move to a variant for the base type.  Checking is_open for every chunk might be wasteful, and certainly doesn't match the intent of what we want.  (will give this more thought)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_connection.hpp",
                    "line": 244,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "nit, looks unrelated?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_connection.hpp",
                    "line": 663,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Keep in mind, what's done here will also need to be done in http2_connection.  I suspect prepareRequestBody might need to go into the Request object itself?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_connection.hpp",
                    "line": 902,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Remove.  This is just the timer for reading headers.  If headers take more than 15 seconds, we have a major issue.\n\nKeep in mind you'll need some re-thinking of the timer system if you're going to read this large file incrementally."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/routing/taggedrule.hpp",
                    "line": 71,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is the wrong place for this logic.\n\ntagged rule should return something like\n\nbool read_as_file();\n\nThen the conection/body logic should deal with the rest accordingly.\n\nroutes that want to read as a file should be registered with something like\n\nBMCWEB_ROUTE(\"....\").readAsFile()....."
                }
            ],
